    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `_NodeReporter.write_captured_output`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_NodeReporter.write_captured_output` function is responsible for writing captured output from a test report to an XML file, adhering to the JUnit XML format. It takes a single parameter, `report`, which is an instance of `TestReport` containing the captured standard output, log, and standard error. The function first checks if the test passed and if logging of passing tests is disabled; if so, it returns early without writing anything. It then determines which types of output to log based on the `logging` attribute of the `xml` object. The function uses the `_prepare_content` helper method to format the captured content with a header and the `_write_content` method to write the formatted content to the XML file under the appropriate tags like `system-out` or `system-err`. If there is any remaining content after processing, it writes it to `system-out`. The function does not handle exceptions or edge cases explicitly, relying on the helper methods to manage any potential issues. It has no side effects other than writing to the XML file.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
